ource":"gtwo_kml_202611211155","idp_filedate":1795262149000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"A large non-tropical low pressure area centered a few hundred miles  east of northeastern Florida continues to produce disorganized  showers and thunderstorms. The system is moving into an  environment that will probably not be conducive for development of  significant tropical or subtropical characteristics.  1. Although the chances of tropical development continue to decrease,  this large weather system is still expected to produce gusty winds,  dangerous surf, rip currents, and coastal flooding along portions  of the southeast and mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into early next week.  In addition, heavy rainfall and the threat of flash flooding will  move northward over coastal portions of the Carolinas and the  mid-Atlantic through the weekend. Interests along the U.S.  East Coast should continue to monitor the progress of this system.  Additional information on this system, including Gale Warnings, can  be found in High Seas Forecasts issued by the National Weather  Service, as well as products issued by your local National Weather  Service forecast office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...10 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...low...10 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}